# DTF tier manifest: backup-restore-completeness
#   (artifact-keeper#5654; fixed on main for 1.12.0 by #5799, not on 1.11.x)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single. The bug is in what the backup archives and
# what the restore reports, not in a storage backend.
#
# THE BUG
# A "full" backup (`POST /api/v1/admin/backups`, then `.../execute`) archived
# 8 tables (users, roles, user_roles, permission_grants, api_tokens,
# repositories, artifacts, download_statistics) and the bytes of the
# `artifacts` rows, and nothing said what it left out:
#   * Docker/OCI images: no oci_blobs / oci_manifests / oci_tags rows and no
#     blob bytes. Restoring a deleted Docker repository answered 200 with
#     `errors: []`, the image then 404ed, and every later boot logged the
#     hollow-tag warning.
#   * groups and members, webhooks, admin settings (system_settings) and scan
#     data (scan_configs, scan_policies, results) never came back.
# Fix #5799: a full backup carries the image rows and blob bytes, groups and
# access, webhooks, settings and scan data; `manifest.json` gains a `contents`
# object (`excluded` categories, `excluded_tables`, `oci_repositories`); the
# restore response gains `status` (`completed` only when `errors` is empty,
# else `partial`) and `excluded`, and `errors` names rows whose bytes the
# archive does not carry.
#
# THE ORACLE (one server: back up, delete, restore on the same instance)
#   setup: a Docker repository with an image pushed through /v2 (config blob,
#   layer blob, manifest tagged v1), a generic repository with 5 artifacts, a
#   group with a member, a global webhook, a system_settings row, a scan
#   config on the Docker repository and a global scan policy. Then a full
#   backup and a metadata backup of the Docker repository.
#   Then the two repositories are deleted through the API (their rows and
#   bytes go), as are the group, the webhook and the scan policy; the setting
#   row is deleted in the database (there is no API to remove a key). Then the
#   full backup is restored.
#   (FIX)      RED on 1.11.13, GREEN on main:
#     * the full backup's manifest.json lists the image, group, webhook,
#       settings and scan tables, names the Docker repository under
#       contents.oci_repositories, and reports what it excludes
#       (contents.excluded with categories, contents.excluded_tables);
#     * after the restore, the image pulls again: the manifest by tag and by
#       digest and both blobs by digest answer 200 with the pushed bytes;
#     * the group (with its member), the webhook and the setting are back;
#     * the Docker repository's scan config and the scan policy are back;
#     * the restore response says `status: completed`, `errors: []`, and
#       lists the excluded categories;
#     * honesty: with the Docker repository deleted again, restoring the
#       metadata backup (rows, no bytes) reports a `status` and lists
#       `artifact_bytes` under `excluded`; pre-fix it answered `errors: []`
#       with nothing excluded, as if the restore were complete. (The
#       `status: partial` path, a full archive whose rows reference bytes it
#       does not carry, needs a corrupted or pre-1.12 archive and is covered
#       by the backend's archive_rows_without_bytes_are_reported_missing_5654
#       and archive_with_oci_manifest_rows_names_the_repository_5654 tests.)
#   (CONTROL)  true on both images: the backup and the restore complete; the
#   image 404s after the delete (so the restore is what brings it back); the
#   5 generic artifacts round-trip with matching SHA-256 checksums.
#
# NOT HERE: restoring into a fresh server (#5856, tier
# backup-restore-fresh-server), a restore whose object pass fails (#5857) and
# backup stream timeouts on a slow store (#5858), both in the restore lane's
# backup-restore-s3-faults tier.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false so a 429 can never masquerade as either verdict.
COVERS="5654"
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
